WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:15.849
RAE:--um, strongly urging

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.550
this wonderful book on all of you.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:25.739
"The White Tiger." How many of you have read this thing?

00:00:25.739 --> 00:00:28.550
É One. Well, did you like it?
STUDENT: I loved it.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.250
RAE: Yeah. It's better than Dickens at

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:37.510
looking through a system from the bottom up and making it entirely real. 

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:38.760
So I strongly urge

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.250
that on you. And once you pick it up, you'll read ite

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.410
in one long session. 

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:50.540
You need a little leisure for that, but 

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:56.030
not a lot. The most-- the simplest thing to say about globalization and the poor

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.400
is that it has-- 

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.660
if you think about poverty in absolute terms, 

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.470
it has alleviated a lot of poverty.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.260
If you think about it in relative terms

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.540
it has made

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.690
a lot of people who

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.700
would not have thought about themselves as poor in

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.790
1840 or 1880 or 1920

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:25.640
 think of themselves as poor now because of what's going on around them:

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.380
the volcanic eruption of wealth 

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.910
around the globe.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:36.270
I should also note there

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.460
that there's a coercive element 

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.360
to economic growth.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.280
Miroslav

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.820
referred to it very briefly.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.460
But even when

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.680
there's no kind of obvious tyranny to the thing,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.640
the marginalization of once--

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.510
once-remunerative and valid 

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:01.020
ways of making a living--

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.610
what happens to the ten-hectare farm

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.250
once the scale economies

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.149
of global commodity production set in,

00:02:12.149 --> 00:02:16.259
is that it's marginalized. It is forced out. 

00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:19.429
And skills that were once valuable become

00:02:19.429 --> 00:02:22.639
less valuable. So there's all that going on. 

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:27.689
But let's do-- this is, once again, Hans Roehsling's [sic] wonderful data animation.

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.319
And I hope I can...

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:32.309
I'm having real trouble with this. This mouse is 

00:02:32.309 --> 00:02:34.930
way too active for me. 

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.799
Here we goÉ We're in 1840 here.

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:43.449
The vertical dimension is life expectancy; the horizontal dimension is income per capita. 

00:02:43.449 --> 00:02:45.249
And the scale on 

00:02:45.249 --> 00:02:48.939
both is linear. So we're not-- lodge scaling

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:51.510
makes it pretty tough to interpret

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.180
change

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.299
so now we're gonna play the movie for

00:02:55.299 --> 00:02:58.259
about 170 years. 

00:02:58.259 --> 00:03:02.629
Each dot is a country. They're color-coded by the map up here. 

00:03:02.629 --> 00:03:06.919
The diameter of the dot represents population. 

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:10.709
And watch what's happening.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:14.669
Let's see if we can stop there for a second. 

00:03:14.669 --> 00:03:20.639
Ok. So far, what's happened, mostly? 

00:03:20.639 --> 00:03:23.150
Life expectancy has gone up

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:23.870
sharply

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.249
and at dramatically different rates, right?

00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:29.209
It's mostly going up.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:31.379
In Western and

00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:33.799
North American settings.

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:35.809
It's going up very fast.

00:03:35.809 --> 00:03:40.349
And in the background, not shown here, the birthrate is not going down. 

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:43.679
So as life expectancy goes up,

00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:45.139
death rates go down,

00:03:45.139 --> 00:03:46.909
birth rates don't go down.

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:53.909
We're creating the population surge which
brings us nearly to 7 billion people today.

00:03:58.509 --> 00:04:05.509
China is the big red balloon.

00:04:06.119 --> 00:04:09.309
OK. So what--

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:16.199
what's the story? The story is:

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:19.309
life expectancy changing

00:04:19.309 --> 00:04:22.150
and then wealth increasing for-- at 

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.099
dramatic rates for about half the globe. 

00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:27.749
What's hidden in this

00:04:27.749 --> 00:04:29.259
is that--

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:33.099
take India, the the large blue bubble--

00:04:33.099 --> 00:04:38.289
is that in India, the vertical scale of inequality is going up fast, fast,

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:39.179
fast.

00:04:39.179 --> 00:04:41.279
And "The White Tiger"

00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:45.389
is that story.

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:48.489
I'm gonna stop there. And it's--

00:04:48.489 --> 00:04:49.939
I think--

00:04:49.939 --> 00:04:52.679
Is it-- I think it's Ambassador Hall's turn, am I right?

